{{Culture of Balisca}}
'''Baliscan cuisine''' reflects local practices and ingredients including influences mainly from the [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|indigenous population]], including the [[W:Mapuche people|Wallche]] and [[Kurupiranga]] cuisine, and cuisines brought by immigrants from Kaftia ([[W:Maghrebi cuisine|Imfezzan cuisine]] and [[W:West African cuisine|West Kaftian cuisine]]), [[Fosia]] ([[W:Chinese cuisine|Darasian cuisine]], [[Sanese cuisine]] and [[W:Indian cuisine|Kemali cuisine]]), [[W:Levantine cuisine|Levantine]] ([[Andalusian cuisine|Andalusian]], [[Bayani cuisine|Bayani]], and [[W:Persian cuisine|Vasarasi cuisine]]), and [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] ([[W:Spanish cuisine|Volisan]], [[W:Italian cuisine|Monsoran]], [[W:Greek cuisine|Aetolian]], and [[Burawa#Cuisine|Burawi]]). Without the familiar ingredients from their home countries, immigrants modified their traditional cuisines by using ingredients available in the [[Northern Triangle]]. Baliscan cuisine is considered one of the world's most prominent examples of [[W:Fusion cuisine|fusion cuisine]].

Baliscan cuisine reflects the country's diverse fauna and flora as well as the cultural traditions of the ethnic groups. Some of the most common ingredients are: cereals such as [[W:Rice|rice]] and [[W:Maize|maize]]; tubers such as potato and [[W:Cassava|cassava]]; assorted [[W:Legume|legumes]]; meats, including beef, chicken, lamb and goat; fish; and seafood. Baliscan cuisine also features a variety of fruits such as [[W:Açaí palm|açaí]], [[W:Physalis peruviana|aguaymanto]], [[W:Cooking banana|plantain]], [[W:Acca sellowiana|quirina]], [[W:Eugenia stipitata|arazá]], [[W:Myrcianthes pungens|guabiyu]], [[W:Purple mangosteen|mangostino]], [[W:Passiflora ligularis|granadilla]], mora ([[W:Blackberry|blackberry]]), [[W:Psidium cattleyanum|strawberry guava]], [[W:Annona muricata|soursop]] and [[W:Passionfruit|passionfruit]]. 

The [[W:National drink|national drink]] of Balisca is [[Café baliscano|''café baliscano'']], made from ''[[W:Coffea arabica|coffea bayanica]]'', the seeds of which have been cultivated in the rich soil of the [[Cabu Verd|Cabu Verd archipelago]] since colonial times. The national cocktail is the [[Guasasa]].

The most visible regional cuisines belong to the regions of [[Mesopotàmia]] and [[Carpinchera]]. Carpinchera's cuisine has significant Fosian (predominately Darasian and [[Zong]]) [[W:Chifa|influence]] in delicacies and dairy products such as ''[[W:Arroz chaufa|Arroz chaufa]]'', ''Kam Lu wantan'' and [[W:Pastel (Brazilian food)|''pastéis'']], while Mesopotàmian cuisine reflects the presence of South Kaftian delicacies such as [[Waji rice]], [[W:Akara|akara]], [[W:Moin moin|''moin moin'']], [[W:Fried plantain|fried plantain]] (called ''[[W:Patacón|patacón]]'' or "dodo")'','' and [[W:Vatapá|vatapa]].

